Same as proposed, minus the jitter (keep l.156 unchanged), with a separate screen-on receiver:
```kotlin
private var lastRxAt = 0L  // onSocketOpen() + onSocketMessage(): lastRxAt = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime()
// Écran rallumé / réseau revenu : les timers Handler (backoff, heartbeat)
// sont gelés pendant la veille profonde, on n'attend pas leur échéance.
private fun kick() {
    if (Prefs.paused) return
    val open = Hub.status == "linked" || Hub.status == "awaiting_link"
    if (ws == null || (open && SystemClock.elapsedRealtime() - lastRxAt > HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_MS)) {
        attempts = 0
        connect()
    }
}
private val netCallback = object : ConnectivityManager.NetworkCallback() {
    override fun onAvailable(network: Network) { Hub.main.post { kick() } }
}
private val screenOn = object : BroadcastReceiver() {
    override fun onReceive(c: Context, i: Intent) { kick() }
}
```
onCreate, after connect(): runCatching { getSystemService(ConnectivityManager::class.java).registerDefaultNetworkCallback(netCallback) }; val f = IntentFilter(Intent.ACTION_SCREEN_ON); if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= 33) registerReceiver(screenOn, f, Context.RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED) else registerReceiver(screenOn, f).
onDestroy, first lines: runCatching { getSystemService(ConnectivityManager::class.java).unregisterNetworkCallback(netCallback) }; runCatching { unregisterReceiver(screenOn) }.
